## What to do instead

- **Don't ask it why.** The 2026 replication<Cite id="young-faith" /> gave 12 current reasoning models a planted hint, confirmed they used it, and checked whether their stated reasoning admitted it: between 40% and 90% of the time, depending on the model, and the number moves ten points depending on who judges. The measurement that started this literature was 25% (Claude 3.7 Sonnet)<Cite id="chen" />. Newer models are better and still a gamble.
- **Don't tell it why.** Models agree with whatever you assert<Cite id="sharma" />; a bare "I don't think that's right" flips correct answers. If you supply the diagnosis and it agrees, you learned nothing.
- **Read the trace.** The truth leaks there. Across the same 12 models, the thinking channel admitted the hint roughly 87% of the time while the final answer admitted it roughly 29%<Cite id="young-div" />; the reverse direction is near zero. But on [Fable 5](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/introducing-claude-fable-5) the trace is the weakest evidence it has ever been: the raw chain of thought is never returned, `display: "summarized"` gets you a model-written summary, and prompting for the raw version is its own refusal category, `reasoning_extraction`.
- **Trust interventions.** Fable 5's own system card<Cite id="card" /> documents confident false completion claims: the model reporting work as verified end-to-end when it only ran static checks. Think a stray TODO caused the bug? Delete it and rerun. An ablation can't confabulate.

